@* Batch G2 decision 6 (multi-select v1, RULED per batch-r-report.md §7): a
   small, dismissible parchment strip -- one removable chip per selected
   node, a count, a "clear." HIDDEN WHEN EMPTY (decision 6, verbatim) --
   this component renders nothing at all rather than an empty shell, the
   same "conditional presence, never an empty placeholder" discipline this
   app's own popover sections already follow. A plain presentational
   component (no state of its own beyond what SelectionTrayService already
   holds) -- MainLayout mounts exactly one instance, on every page, so the
   tray itself persists across reader<->world navigation the same way the
   header does. Purposely carries the SAME parchment material every other
   shared artifact in this app uses regardless of page theme (the popover's
   own "One popover style everywhere," design-direction.md) -- batch-r-
   report.md §7's own "parchment strip" proposal, verbatim. NO ACTION VERBS
   here (decision 6's own explicit scoping) -- every chip does exactly one
   thing, remove itself; there is no per-chip "do something with this node"
   control at all. *@
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore
@using BibleAtlas.Client.State
@inject StateAtom<IReadOnlyList<ExplorationDescriptor>> SelectionAtom
@implements IDisposable

@* Batch ST-3 (R2): renders a PROJECTION of the shared Selection atom --
   Items below reads SelectionAtom.Value directly (never a copy); every
   control dispatches (RemoveSelection/ClearSelection) instead of calling a
   service method. SelectionTrayService itself is no longer injected here --
   this component never needed Available (decision 6 never asked the TRAY
   to hide on storage unavailability, only the SAVE affordance elsewhere). *@
@{
    var items = SelectionAtom.Value;
}
@if (items.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="selection-tray" data-testid="selection-tray">
        <span class="selection-tray-count" data-testid="selection-tray-count">
            @items.Count selected
        </span>
        <ul class="selection-tray-chips">
            @for (var i = 0; i < items.Count; i++)
            {
                var item = items[i];
                <li>
                    <span class="selection-chip" data-testid="@($"selection-chip-{i}")">
                        <span class="selection-chip-kind">@item.Kind</span>
                        <span class="selection-chip-title">@item.Title</span>
                        <button type="button" class="selection-chip-remove" aria-label="@($"Remove {item.Title} from selection")"
                                @onclick="() => SelectionAtom.Dispatch(new RemoveSelection(item))">&times;</button>
                    </span>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
        <button type="button" class="selection-clear" data-testid="selection-clear" @onclick="() => SelectionAtom.Dispatch(new ClearSelection())">Clear</button>
    </div>
}

@code {
    // Batch ST-3 (R2): the Selection atom is a DI singleton shared with
    // every page's own Ctrl/Cmd-click gesture handler (Reader.razor's
    // VerseLine wiring, World.razor's OnPlaceToggleSelect) -- a dispatch
    // made from EITHER never automatically re-renders THIS separate
    // component instance on its own (Blazor only re-renders a component in
    // response to ITS OWN parameter changes or explicit StateHasChanged),
    // so this subscribes to the atom's own Changed event (law 5, agreement,
    // is what GUARANTEES this subscription is enough -- every projection of
    // one atom reads the same Value at read time, so there's nothing left
    // to keep separately in sync).
    protected override void OnInitialized() => SelectionAtom.Changed += OnChanged;

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose() => SelectionAtom.Changed -= OnChanged;
}
